(7697819, 1262773, 30214, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by streetpigeon:</b></div><hr/><i> Fine grungy, HDR-ish image.  The structures and interwoven gizmos have that deadly serious mien of assault and implacable degradation, even as it''s mission is eco-friendly.  Can this substitute itself for an horizon of distant mountains?  But even more so, it''s the sky, these boiling clouds again a parody of nature.  The photo is done so well that we can still appreciate the intricate beauty of the beast.  Gives one pause amongst deeper discomfort.<br/>Co- personal favorite of the challenge. </i></td></tr></table> Hi Rich,<br/>thank you for your nice comment on my photo! Yes, this whole place, Salton Sea, has a very weird, disquieting feel.  Everything there, even this relatively new geothermal plant, show clear signs of decay. I don''t know if you know it''s history, it is fascinating. There is a nice documentary about it, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8TjGAWxL23c" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Plagues &amp; Pleasures on the Salton Sea</a></td>', 'Originally posted by streetpigeon: Fine grungy, HDR-ish image.  The structures and interwoven gizmos have that deadly serious mien of assault and implacable degradation, even as it''s mission is eco-friendly.  Can this substitute itself for an horizon of distant mountains?  But even more so, it''s the sky, these boiling clouds again a parody of nature.  The photo is done so well that we can still appreciate the intricate beauty of the beast.  Gives one pause amongst deeper discomfort.Co- personal favorite of the challenge.  Hi Rich,thank you for your nice comment on my photo! Yes, this whole place, Salton Sea, has a very weird, disquieting feel.  Everything there, even this relatively new geothermal plant, show clear signs of decay. I don''t know if you know it''s history, it is fascinating. There is a nice documentary about it, Plagues & Pleasures on the Salton Sea', '2021-07-21 15:41:04.000000', NULL, 0),
